    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>KV62 is smaller and less decorated than other royal tombs of its time and probably began as a tomb for a non-royal person, so its 5,398 objects had to be packed densely into the space.</li><li>Robbers entered twice within a few years of the burial, but the tomb was resealed and then buried under debris, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>'s mummy and most of the goods stayed intact.</li><li>Howard Carter and Lord Carnarvon found it in 1922; the clearance took ten digging seasons, and most of the contents went to the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> rather than being divided with the sponsors.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Among the furniture was a foldable bed, the only intact example known from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and a reed staff labelled as one that the king &quot;cut with his own hand&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

